5 378:37 New Hampshire Energy Policy. The general court declares that it shall be the energy
6 policy of this state to meet the energy needs of the citizens and businesses of the state at the lowest
7 reasonable cost while providing for the reliability and diversity of energy sources; to maximize the
8 use of cost effective energy efficiency and other demand side resources; and to protect the safety and
9 health of the citizens, the physical environment of the state, and the future supplies of resources,
10 with consideration of the financial stability of the state's utilities. It is the policy of the sovereign
11 state of New Hampshire and purpose of this chapter to use market and regulatory
12 mechanisms to promote affordable, reliable, diverse, and secure energy resources for the
13 publichealth,safety,andwelfareofitscitizensanditsphysicalenvironment.
14 I. New Hampshire shall promote the development of resources to achieve the
15 purpose of this chapter, fostering a range of technology types, including reliable, on-
16 demand, diverse, and firm energy resources, and distributed energy sources and storage
17 capacitiesconsistentwiththesegoals,whileallowingforcustomerchoice.
18 II. New Hampshire shall promote the development of resources, tools, and
19 infrastructure to enhance the state's ability to ensure the state’s energy independence by
20 removingregulatory barriersto innovationto ensure thatthe statecanprocure affordable,
21 reliable, diverse, and secure energy resources, including energy generation combined with
22 storage,andconsistentwithRSA362-F.
23 III. New Hampshire shall allow market forces and market-based mechanisms to
24 driveprudentuseofenergyresources. Governmentinterventiontoeconomicallyadvantage
25 one technology over another shall be time-limited, narrow, or necessary to achieve a
26 specificpolicygoal.
27 IV. New Hampshire shall pursue energy conservation and efficiency according to
28 market principles in order to address market barriers, as regulated by policies for the
29 public good and public health, and focus on market transformation in accordance with
30 cost-effective fiscal strategies as authorized by the legislature, and consistent with RSA
31 374-F.
